PREFIX pair:  <http://w3id.org/airiskkg/pair-ai#>
PREFIX facet: <http://w3id.org/airiskkg/facets#>
PREFIX dpv:   <https://w3id.org/dpv#>

# Bridge: "this element holds personal data" -> pair:SensitiveInformation.
#
# The modeler annotates the kind of personal data with DPV concepts, which is
# what Rule R3 asks for - reference DPV, never mirror it. Those annotations were
# inert: no condition read facet:hasPersonalDataCategory, so declaring a store to
# hold health records changed no outcome. This bridges the facet into the one
# vocabulary the propagation engine and the conditions already speak, so the
# annotation reaches a finding without a second propagating vocabulary (R8: Data
# Category is the one facet that is also derived).
#
# Deliberately collapsing: every kind of personal data becomes
# pair:SensitiveInformation. Health and financial data do not get separate
# categories, because minting them would mirror DPV's taxonomy into
# pair:DataCategoryScheme, which R3 forbids. Specificity is preserved where it
# was asserted - the DPV value stays on the element - and is recoverable from
# there if a condition ever needs it.
#
# The exclusion is the point of care. The property ranges over any DPV concept,
# including the ones that say the data is NOT personal, and a blanket rule would
# mark anonymised data as sensitive - the exact opposite of what the annotation
# says. DPV settles which those are by its own axioms: dpv:AnonymisedData is a
# subclass of dpv:NonPersonalData, while dpv:PseudonymisedData is a subclass of
# dpv:PersonalData and so is correctly left in. The list is explicit because DPV
# is referenced rather than loaded, so there is no subclass hierarchy here to
# walk (verified against the DPV 2.1 serialization, 2026-08-11).

CONSTRUCT {
    ?element pair:containsDataCategory pair:SensitiveInformation .
}
WHERE {
    ?element facet:hasPersonalDataCategory ?personalDataKind .

    FILTER (?personalDataKind NOT IN (dpv:AnonymisedData, dpv:NonPersonalData))
}
